    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d646">

                <pb facs="0636" n="624" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg624"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/867: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-03-22T17:00:00+00:00">March 22, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 22—12:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>161. A journalist has just informed me Ambassador Yoshida had told him this
                    morning that in spite of every desire on the part of Sato<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d646-f1"/> to meet the English, nevertheless, due to strength of the
                    naval party, Japan will not accept 14-inch gun calibre limitation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d646-f1" n="16" xml:id="d646-f1-content">
                <p>Naotake Sato, Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs, replacing Hayashi who
                    replaced Arita.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
